that there was a  very good possibility of the third wave  and it looks like  that's what we're seeing alberta's  government has been facing tremendous  pressure to ease restrictions  at least two united conservative caucus  members took to social media monday  urging the province to proceed to step  three of reopening  but alberta's health minister says  making that move comes with too  much risk alberta now sits at 280  covet hospitalizations which is a rise  of 16  from a week ago and this is a problem  and a warning sign that we must take  seriously more than 80 percent of  alberta covet patients in icu  right now are under 65 years old under  the province's reopening framework the  next step would include easing  restrictions on things like indoor  gatherings  bingo halls and adult team sports but  tyler chandra says  for now those measures will have to wait  there will be no easing of any  restrictions at this time  this is the safe move it's the smart  move to  make for our province right now and it's  absolutely necessary  to help us avoid a third wave that would  take more lives  and once again put more pressure on the  hospital system  it's disappointing news for businesses  hoping to reopen again  but with covet cases surging dr james  talbot agrees it's the  right move to make our fear which is  based in reality is that we're going to  see  the second wave on steroids heather urex  west global news calgary  tonight's nhl game between the edmonton  oilers and the montreal canadiens has  been postponed because of covet fears  two montreal players are now on the  league's covet protocol list  they may not have the virus but may have  come in contact with someone who does  it's the first time a match-up in the  league's north division has been called  off because of covet the spring break  hangover in miami beach  coming up the curfew to control the  crowds  confidence in the astrazeneca vaccine  may be restored with new trial data out  of the u.s  showing it is safe and effective a study  completed by astrazeneca shows the  two-shot vaccine developed by oxford  university  is 79 effective against covet 19.  it also offered strong protection for  older people  who weren't well represented in earlier  studies  this study involved more than 30 000  participants  and scientists found there was no  increased risk of clots  that was a concern for several european  countries which temporarily suspended  the use of the vaccine the u.s is  bracing for what could be a fourth wave  of covid19 infections  case numbers are climbing and there are  fears more contagious variants will  spread rapidly  as millions of spring break travelers  head home from hot spots  like florida jackson proskow reports  the party is over in miami beach  where the mayor declared a state of  emergency after large  maskless groups swarmed city streets  at one point a police swat team tried to  break up the crowds  a nightly 8pm curfew is now in effect  until the end of spring break  it's a little ridiculous considering  that you know we are paying to stay on  the hotels on the strip and we can't  even have dinner  many came here looking for a vacation  from the virus  drawn in by florida's lacks restrictions  governor ron desantis is making the free  state  nomads we can come and have some good  but kovid hasn't taken  time off florida is the u.s hot spot for  cases of the more contagious variant  first detected in the uk the young  people out partying  are less likely to have been vaccinated  the likelihood of these individuals  contracting  and spreading the virus amongst  themselves is relatively high  when they then go back to their home  states they are  introducing the virus in cases are  already on the rise in at least 21  states including michigan  which is reporting more than a 50  percent increase in cases in just the  last week  we are a critical point in this pandemic  a fork in the road  it's not just virus variants from afar  similar home-grown mutations have sprung  up in california and new york and are  gaining steam  we must act now and i am worried that if  we don't take the right actions now  we will have another avoidable surge and  so the race is on to vaccinate  one in four americans has now received  at least their first dose  two months from now there will be enough  available for everyone in the country  until then the virus will continue to  pose a risk  even to those who ignore it jackson  prosco global news  washington still ahead feel the fire in  iceland  the eruption centuries in the making  the worst flooding in 60 years has  sparked a natural disaster in australia  about four months of rain have fallen in  a matter of days  at least 18 000 people have been forced  to flee  from the air new south wales looks like  the ocean swallowed it up  there's water as far as the eye can see  the situation is staggering  stretching from sydney to brisbane towns  and suburbs are already completely cut  off  and more rain is in the forecast a huge  fire has ripped through a rohingya  refugee camp in bangladesh killing at  least three people but it's feared  more could be dead  the flames erupted in the early morning  and took more than four hours to bring  under control  the cause is under investigation this  area of bangladesh grew into the world's  largest refugee camp in 2017  after more than 700 000 rohingya muslims  escaped a violent crackdown in  neighboring myanmar  up next meet the canadian woman hoping  to make aviation history  on the red planet  take a look at this spectacular view of  an erupting volcano in iceland the  dramatic drone footage shows lava  spewing and flowing  into rivers experts estimate about 300  000 cubic meters of lava have poured out  so far  thousands of people are flocking to the  site for a rare up-close look  no volcanic ash has been detected but  there are high levels of volcanic gases  the eruption which began friday is the  first in the area  in more than 800 years nasa's  perseverance rover is preparing to show  off  more of its moves on mars within the  rover's belly is the ingenuity  helicopter which nasa considers a  high-risk high-reward technology  it will become the first man-made device  to fly on another planet  during its maiden flight next month  tonight mike delay speaks to the  canadian engineer turned  rocket star playing a crucial role in  the mission  history is filled with the impossible in  1903 the wright brothers  changed the world and in a few weeks  nasa will try and change space  exploration  with its own moment of flight on mars  246 million kilometers from kitty hawk  north carolina i think so i mean we're  flying on another planet come on  like you know it's kind of it's kind of  crazy because  you know we we've only been flying on  earth for  for about a you know 100 years and now  we're saying we're going to go to  another planet where gravity is  different  ingenuity is its name and it's apropos  nasa had to design a helicopter that  could fly an atmosphere 99  thinner than on earth and survive minus  90 degrees celsius temperatures at night  after years of testing all that's left  is to prove it can fly on the red planet  what a journey to get to this point for  ingenuity and  systems engineer farah alibay i think  the best description is meandering for  my path and eventually i made it and i  made it where i wanted to be  mostly because this was my dream and now  the montreal-born  cambridge educated granddaughter of  immigrants who fled civil war in  madagascar  has become one of the new faces of nasa  but i think what we forget to talk about  is is all the failures along the way all  the sacrifices that had to happen  all of the wrong turns or the detours  that were taken  life certainly wasn't easy for alabama  growing up as a minority in quebec as a  francophone in england and overcoming  nasa's lack of diversity  she applied to the agency over 50 times  eventually getting noticed by the right  people  hi sophie it's why she relishes the  opportunity to speak with young girls  how long does the helicopter  fly so for our first flight we're not  gonna go very far we're actually just  hopping up and down  and so we're gonna fly about 40 seconds  to one minute right so the same way as  like  you don't learn to run first you walk  first so crawl that's what we're doing  alabay is a genius at this stuff but  it's the way she speaks to an  eight-year-old  that makes her a rocket star mike carley  global news toronto  that's a great story and that is global  national for this monday  i'm robin gill tonight your canada is  pishu falls manitoba  please keep sending your photos to  viewers at globalnational.com  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

